WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.800
Welcome to Day 9: Events, Meetings, CLE and Reputation.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:10.525
We'll start with events and travel logistics, the biggest block today,

00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:13.662
then move on to meetings and video conferencing.

00:00:13.662 --> 00:00:17.475
After that comes compliance tracking and professional development,

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:22.175
then stress and wellbeing, and we'll close with reputation and brand.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.388
By the end of today, you'll be able to run events end to end,

00:00:26.388 --> 00:00:32.138
invites, intake forms and CLE included, keep meetings and compliance tracking on

00:00:32.138 --> 00:00:35.300
schedule, manage stress in a high-pressure role,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.150
and protect the organization's reputation.

00:00:40.075 --> 00:00:43.825
Section 1 of 5: Events and Travel Logistics.

00:00:43.825 --> 00:00:48.300
We'll run an event end to end, manage CLE for firm-hosted events,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.025
and handle high-stakes travel disruption.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.888
A headcount tells you 58 people are coming.

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:59.825
A list by name tells you that the client who matters most never finished

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:01.012
registering.

00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:04.175
The whole event runs on that kind of detail.

00:01:04.975 --> 00:01:08.375
First, track who has registered by name from day one.

00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:12.200
Next, plan who's attending what and what materials they need.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.100
Then, follow up with anyone whose registration is incomplete,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.725
while there's still time.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.400
After that, compare the registration list with who actually came.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.650
Finally, write down what went wrong, so the next event is better.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.600
Try this one.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.425
Thorne & Partners is hosting a client appreciation evening for 60 guests in ten

00:01:34.425 --> 00:01:35.387
weeks.

00:01:35.387 --> 00:01:39.250
What's done by week eight, by week four and by the day before?

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.288
Events look effortless when three simple documents are doing the work:

00:01:45.288 --> 00:01:48.938
a master checklist with an owner and date for every task,

00:01:48.938 --> 00:01:51.875
a run sheet that lays out the day minute by minute,

00:01:51.875 --> 00:01:55.900
and a contact sheet with every vendor and helper's phone number.

00:01:56.725 --> 00:02:01.250
Most event problems are predictable, like a late caterer or a projector that won't

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.238
connect.

00:02:02.238 --> 00:02:07.000
On the day, we're the calm center: we know where everything is and who to call.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:12.875
First, we build the checklist from a template, working back from the date.

00:02:12.875 --> 00:02:18.312
Next, the run sheet: times, who leads each part, what it needs and what's next.

00:02:18.312 --> 00:02:21.875
Then we confirm every vendor 48 hours before.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.837
After that, we pack a day-of kit: printed sheets,

00:02:26.837 --> 00:02:31.825
spare badges, chargers, tape, first aid and the slides on a USB.

00:02:31.825 --> 00:02:36.125
Finally, we walk the venue before guests arrive, and debrief within a week.

00:02:36.875 --> 00:02:38.075
Picture this.

00:02:38.075 --> 00:02:40.750
The client breakfast starts at 8:00.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:46.150
At 7:20 the caterer hasn't arrived, the projector won't connect to Elias's laptop,

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.925
and two guests are early.

00:02:47.925 --> 00:02:52.575
Using your run sheet and contact sheet, what do you do in the next 10 minutes?

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.512
A single wrong detail in an invite can make a whole meeting fail.

00:02:58.512 --> 00:03:01.787
A good invite answers five questions at a glance:

00:03:01.787 --> 00:03:05.975
what it is, when it is with the time zone, where or how to join,

00:03:05.975 --> 00:03:08.125
who's needed and what to prepare.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.400
Event invitations follow a rhythm: a save-the-date,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.750
then the invitation with an RSVP date, reminders,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.275
and final details the day before.

00:03:20.225 --> 00:03:25.050
First, we check availability, then send one invite, not a chain of emails.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.462
Next, a clear title, the right time zone, and the link,

00:03:28.462 --> 00:03:30.937
dial-in and location in the invite.

00:03:30.937 --> 00:03:35.575
Then a short agenda, anything to read, and who's required or optional.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.350
After that, for events: save-the-date six to eight weeks out,

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:45.700
invitation three to four weeks out, reminders at a week and a day.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.275
Finally, we update the existing invite when things change,

00:03:49.275 --> 00:03:51.250
and cancel through the calendar.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.762
Take a second with this one.

00:03:53.762 --> 00:03:56.962
Elias wants a call next week with a client in London,

00:03:56.962 --> 00:04:00.275
a partner in New York and an expert in Denver.

00:04:00.275 --> 00:04:02.950
How would you show the time so nobody gets it wrong,

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.050
and what do you check before sending?

00:04:06.975 --> 00:04:11.150
A good intake form means the event or the first meeting starts with everything you

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.350
need, instead of a scramble.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:18.100
It asks only what's needed, in plain words, with the required fields clear,

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.775
because every extra question means fewer people finish it.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:26.375
And forms often collect personal or confidential information,

00:04:26.375 --> 00:04:28.912
so they're sent and stored securely.

00:04:28.912 --> 00:04:32.950
For new clients, the answers may also feed the conflict check.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:39.275
First, we use the firm's approved form tool, and the attorney approves client intake

00:04:39.275 --> 00:04:40.450
questions.

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:46.112
Next, for events: name, organization, email, dietary and accessibility needs,

00:04:46.112 --> 00:04:48.725
sessions, and bar number and state for CLE.

00:04:48.725 --> 00:04:53.012
Then, for new clients: contact details, everyone involved,

00:04:53.012 --> 00:04:57.400
key dates and a short description, with documents through secure upload.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:04.225
After that, we send it with a short note: why, the deadline and who to ask.

00:05:04.225 --> 00:05:06.025
We test the link first.

00:05:06.025 --> 00:05:10.800
Finally, we track completions, send one reminder and move the answers into the right

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:11.825
system.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.175
Here's one to try.

00:05:14.175 --> 00:05:18.525
Elias is meeting a potential new client, a small construction company,

00:05:18.525 --> 00:05:22.775
on Monday, and wants their details and everyone involved in their dispute

00:05:22.775 --> 00:05:23.962
beforehand.

00:05:23.962 --> 00:05:27.700
Pause the video and draft the six questions on your intake form,

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.525
and the note you send with it.

00:05:31.425 --> 00:05:36.450
When the firm hosts a CLE event, attendees are counting on us for credit they need

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.312
to keep their licenses.

00:05:38.312 --> 00:05:42.625
The firm is the course provider, and it has duties to the state bars that approve

00:05:42.625 --> 00:05:43.700
the credit.

00:05:44.475 --> 00:05:49.138
Every state has its own rules: whether the course needs approval in advance,

00:05:49.138 --> 00:05:53.688
how many minutes make an hour of credit, which categories like ethics apply,

00:05:53.688 --> 00:05:55.450
and what records we keep.

00:05:57.425 --> 00:06:01.675
First, months ahead, we find out which states attendees are licensed in and check

00:06:01.675 --> 00:06:04.175
each state's rules and deadlines.

00:06:04.175 --> 00:06:07.788
Next, we prepare the application: a timed agenda,

00:06:07.788 --> 00:06:12.150
speaker bios, materials and the hours and categories requested.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:16.275
Then we track attendance properly: sign-in and sign-out sheets,

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:20.025
or the webinar's attendance report and any required codes.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.412
After that, we issue certificates with the name,

00:06:24.412 --> 00:06:29.525
course, date, provider, hours, category and any approval number.

00:06:29.525 --> 00:06:33.900
Finally, we report to the states that require it and keep the file as long as each

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.150
state says.

00:06:35.925 --> 00:06:37.662
Here's a situation.

00:06:37.662 --> 00:06:40.975
The firm is hosting a two-hour webinar in six weeks,

00:06:40.975 --> 00:06:44.838
with one hour of ethics, for attendees licensed in New York,

00:06:44.838 --> 00:06:46.950
New Jersey and California.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.925
What do you do this week, what do you collect at registration,

00:06:49.925 --> 00:06:51.825
and what happens afterwards?

00:06:53.725 --> 00:06:58.138
When the executive is speaking at an event, the calendar entry is the smallest part

00:06:58.138 --> 00:06:59.288
of your job.

00:06:59.288 --> 00:07:05.225
Organizers have their own deadlines for bios, headshots, slides and technical needs.

00:07:05.225 --> 00:07:08.662
Miss one, and the speaking slot itself can be at risk.

00:07:08.662 --> 00:07:11.050
So you run it like a mini project.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.588
First, make one checklist per speaking engagement,

00:07:14.588 --> 00:07:17.900
travel included, each item with its own due date.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:22.900
Next, confirm the format early: keynote, panel or fireside chat.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.625
Finally, before the event, send the executive one briefing:

00:07:26.625 --> 00:07:30.475
the time, the format, who else is speaking and what's expected.

00:07:31.225 --> 00:07:32.838
Take a second with this one.

00:07:32.838 --> 00:07:37.775
Elias's slides are due Friday, and on Thursday he's still changing them.

00:07:37.775 --> 00:07:41.650
You also learn the venue projector only takes one type of cable.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.825
What do you send, and to whom?

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:48.150
A sponsorship is a real contract.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:51.888
When the firm sponsors an event, it's owed specific things:

00:07:51.888 --> 00:07:56.950
its logo in certain places, maybe a speaking slot or the attendee list.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:01.425
Noticing something's missing on the day is far better than noticing afterwards.

00:08:02.225 --> 00:08:06.500
First, before the event, check each promised item against what's actually being

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.600
provided.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.725
Next, keep the signed agreement with you during the event,

00:08:10.725 --> 00:08:12.550
in case there's a dispute.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:17.000
Finally, track what the sponsorship cost against the value it delivered.

00:08:17.775 --> 00:08:18.975
Try this one.

00:08:18.975 --> 00:08:22.150
The firm's logo is missing from the printed program,

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.550
even though the agreement guarantees it.

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:28.175
What do you do in the moment, and what do you follow up on afterwards?

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:33.988
Moderating a live event means watching actively for problems,

00:08:33.988 --> 00:08:35.938
not waiting for them to happen.

00:08:35.938 --> 00:08:39.975
Your job is to keep it running smoothly for the audience and the speaker,

00:08:39.975 --> 00:08:42.000
without distracting the speaker.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.975
First, before it starts, confirm the executive's login and check their audio and

00:08:46.975 --> 00:08:48.000
video.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.850
Next, during the event, watch for sound drops, screen-share failures and questions

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:54.500
to pass to the speaker.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.788
Then, for sessions that count toward professional credits,

00:08:57.788 --> 00:09:00.350
track attendance and times carefully.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:05.325
Finally, afterwards, confirm certificates and records, and document the session.

00:09:06.075 --> 00:09:07.113
Picture this.

00:09:07.113 --> 00:09:11.713
Halfway through Elias's webinar, 30 attendees drop off and chat fills with 'no

00:09:11.713 --> 00:09:15.150
sound.' Elias keeps talking, not aware.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.475
What do you do in the next 60 seconds?

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:22.650
An event's real value comes from what happens after it.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:26.875
Without follow-up, a connection made there fades just like any other.

00:09:27.675 --> 00:09:31.850
First, make notes during the event on who you met and what you talked about.

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:38.050
Next, follow up within 48 to 72 hours, while they still remember the conversation.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:42.350
Finally, record who attended and what came of it in your normal relationship

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:43.800
tracking system.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:45.975
Think about this one.

00:09:45.975 --> 00:09:49.975
The firm spent $12,000 on three conferences this year,

00:09:49.975 --> 00:09:54.325
and Elias asks, 'Were any of them worth it?' What would you need to have tracked to

00:09:54.325 --> 00:09:55.450
answer him?

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:01.650
When travel falls apart, the real problem is rarely the flight itself.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:05.975
It's the deposition, the closing or the board meeting at the other end.

00:10:05.975 --> 00:10:09.650
So you triage: what has to happen, and in what order?

00:10:10.475 --> 00:10:14.650
First, identify the fixed point: the one commitment he can't miss.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:17.975
Next, check the airline app while you're on the phone.

00:10:17.975 --> 00:10:21.175
Apps often show options faster than agents.

00:10:21.175 --> 00:10:24.988
Then, if no direct rebooking works, try another airport,

00:10:24.988 --> 00:10:27.700
another airline or a car for the final stretch,

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:31.250
and warn the other side early if a delay is unavoidable.

00:10:33.175 --> 00:10:36.850
After that, send Elias one clear message: what happened,

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.750
what you're doing and anything he needs to decide.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.338
Finally, once it's fixed, update everyone downstream:

00:10:43.338 --> 00:10:46.050
drivers, hotels and the meeting host.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.363
Take a second with this one.

00:10:48.363 --> 00:10:51.675
Elias's connection to a closing-day meeting is cancelled,

00:10:51.675 --> 00:10:53.438
and the meeting can't move.

00:10:53.438 --> 00:10:58.312
Option one: a red-eye on another airline, landing two hours before.

00:10:58.312 --> 00:11:03.638
Option two: a private car for the last leg, costing much more but letting him sleep.

00:11:03.638 --> 00:11:06.175
How do you decide, and how do you present it?

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.250
Section 2 of 5: Meetings and Video Conferencing.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:15.575
We'll cover everyday meeting notes, board meetings and minutes,

00:11:15.575 --> 00:11:19.750
and running video calls, from platform admin to troubleshooting.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:25.100
A meeting is only as useful as what people do afterwards,

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:26.925
and your notes decide that.

00:11:26.925 --> 00:11:30.150
Good notes capture three things: what was decided,

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.750
what needs doing, and what's still open.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:37.162
And notes from meetings about legal matters may be privileged or confidential,

00:11:37.162 --> 00:11:39.950
so we label them and share them carefully.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.538
First, before the meeting, we confirm the agenda and set up a template:

00:11:44.538 --> 00:11:48.425
attendees, decisions, actions and open questions.

00:11:48.425 --> 00:11:51.463
Next, we write decisions as clear statements.

00:11:51.463 --> 00:11:55.025
Then we write each action as a verb, an owner and a date.

00:11:55.025 --> 00:11:58.500
Without an owner and a date, it usually doesn't happen.

00:12:00.425 --> 00:12:05.300
After that, we send the notes within 24 hours, with actions at the top.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:10.325
Finally, we add the actions to our tracker and check them before the next meeting.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:12.538
Here's one to try.

00:12:12.538 --> 00:12:17.863
After a 45-minute Harlow strategy call, your notes say: 'Discussed deposition.

00:12:17.863 --> 00:12:19.113
Expert maybe.

00:12:19.113 --> 00:12:20.438
Budget concerns.

00:12:20.438 --> 00:12:25.075
Elias to think about it.' Pause the video and rewrite them so someone who missed the

00:12:25.075 --> 00:12:27.875
call knows exactly what happens next.

00:12:29.825 --> 00:12:34.275
Board minutes record what was decided and by whom, not who argued what.

00:12:34.275 --> 00:12:37.850
Most of a board meeting's work happens before anyone sits down:

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:39.950
the agenda and the papers.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:43.925
And the minutes afterwards are a legal record, not a transcript.

00:12:43.925 --> 00:12:47.800
Both need precision, discretion and getting it right first time,

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:50.225
because board mistakes are costly.

00:12:52.175 --> 00:12:55.850
First, confirm the agenda with the executive, and often the chair,

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:59.525
well ahead, and send papers early enough to be read.

00:12:59.525 --> 00:13:03.350
Next, put the board pack together the same way every time.

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:06.900
Then, during the meeting, capture who's present and absent,

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:10.400
each motion, who seconded it and the exact result.

00:13:12.325 --> 00:13:16.675
After that, write the minutes promptly, in neutral, factual language.

00:13:16.675 --> 00:13:21.050
Finally, send the draft round for corrections before it's finalized.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:22.725
Picture this.

00:13:22.725 --> 00:13:27.062
Your draft minutes say, 'Director Harlow strongly objected to the budget and was

00:13:27.062 --> 00:13:30.750
overruled after a heated debate.' How would you rewrite it,

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:32.200
and why does it matter?

00:13:34.125 --> 00:13:37.863
If an annual shareholder meeting doesn't have enough people present,

00:13:37.863 --> 00:13:39.838
none of its decisions count.

00:13:39.838 --> 00:13:43.850
An AGM has formal rules beyond a normal board meeting:

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:48.725
legally required notice periods, a minimum number of attendees called a quorum,

00:13:48.725 --> 00:13:50.800
and often a bigger guest list.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:55.912
First, confirm the required notice period for this meeting and jurisdiction well in

00:13:55.912 --> 00:13:57.075
advance.

00:13:57.075 --> 00:14:00.950
Next, track RSVPs against the quorum requirement.

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:04.725
Finally, prepare the pack with the same care as a board meeting,

00:14:04.725 --> 00:14:07.525
plus any proxy and voting materials.

00:14:08.275 --> 00:14:10.088
Take a second with this one.

00:14:10.088 --> 00:14:14.500
On the morning of the AGM, a shareholder turns up with a handwritten proxy for

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:18.412
someone else, and the procedure doesn't say whether that's accepted.

00:14:18.412 --> 00:14:21.850
Who decides, and what should have been settled earlier?

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:28.162
At executive level, small slips stand out: an open microphone,

00:14:28.162 --> 00:14:31.213
a late join or an unprepared executive.

00:14:31.213 --> 00:14:35.825
Etiquette here isn't stiffness; it's removing friction so the conversation gets full

00:14:35.825 --> 00:14:36.875
attention.

00:14:36.875 --> 00:14:39.825
And how you show up reflects on the executive.

00:14:41.775 --> 00:14:44.638
First, give the executive a short note before the call:

00:14:44.638 --> 00:14:47.775
agenda, length, who's attending and their roles.

00:14:47.775 --> 00:14:51.150
Next, join a few minutes early and check the technology works,

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.375
so problems get found before the executive arrives.

00:14:54.375 --> 00:14:57.800
Then, keep your own presence professional and low-key.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:01.463
Finally, handle the mechanics quietly, like muting noise,

00:15:01.463 --> 00:15:03.975
sharing documents and watching the chat.

00:15:04.725 --> 00:15:05.963
Try this one.

00:15:05.963 --> 00:15:09.725
Thirty seconds before Elias's call with a prospective client,

00:15:09.725 --> 00:15:14.075
you notice the invite used the wrong time zone and the client may have been waiting

00:15:14.075 --> 00:15:15.225
for an hour.

00:15:15.225 --> 00:15:16.775
What do you do right now?

00:15:18.725 --> 00:15:23.550
Set up video calls properly beforehand, and most problems never happen.

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:26.113
Running the platform means owning the settings,

00:15:26.113 --> 00:15:29.538
and Zoom, Teams and Meet each work a little differently,

00:15:29.538 --> 00:15:31.725
so know your firm's main one well.

00:15:32.525 --> 00:15:35.463
First, before scheduling, confirm the key settings:

00:15:35.463 --> 00:15:39.225
waiting room, screen sharing, recording and registration.

00:15:39.225 --> 00:15:44.875
Next, use a pre-meeting checklist: test access, check logins and attach the agenda.

00:15:44.875 --> 00:15:48.025
Then, know how to manage people live: mute, remove,

00:15:48.025 --> 00:15:50.600
promote to co-host and switch screens.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.325
Finally, review the account's default settings regularly.

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:56.813
Take a second with this one.

00:15:56.813 --> 00:16:01.000
A client webinar for 200 people is set up like an internal meeting,

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.100
and anyone can unmute and share their screen.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.338
Someone shares something inappropriate.

00:16:06.338 --> 00:16:08.100
What should the settings have been?

00:16:09.975 --> 00:16:13.850
When a call breaks, get it working first and work out why afterwards.

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:17.175
Most video problems fall into a few predictable groups:

00:16:17.175 --> 00:16:20.613
login, audio and video, and screen sharing.

00:16:20.613 --> 00:16:24.975
A calm, step-by-step approach beats panic nearly every time.

00:16:26.925 --> 00:16:30.200
First, for a login failure, check the link and the browser,

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:32.675
then contact the platform's support.

00:16:32.675 --> 00:16:36.938
Next, for sound or picture problems, check the right device is selected,

00:16:36.938 --> 00:16:40.250
check permissions, then try leaving and rejoining.

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:44.700
Then, before any important call, have a backup ready, like a phone number.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.750
Finally, record every problem, the fix and how long it took.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.213
Here's a situation.

00:16:51.213 --> 00:16:54.750
A client can hear everyone, but no one can hear the client.

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:58.725
Walk through the checks in order, and what you say while you're fixing it.

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:05.300
Section 3 of 5: Compliance Tracking and Professional Development.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:10.800
We'll cover CLE tracking, membership renewals, and ethics and gift compliance.

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:16.225
Four simple procedures keep professional development on track,

00:17:16.225 --> 00:17:19.038
and each one prevents a specific failure.

00:17:19.038 --> 00:17:24.250
They cover event registration, attendance, team training and reputation.

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:30.575
First, for registrations, check the provider is legitimate and approved before

00:17:30.575 --> 00:17:31.625
booking.

00:17:31.625 --> 00:17:36.225
Next, for attendance, save certificates in one audit-ready folder.

00:17:36.225 --> 00:17:41.025
Then, for team training, assess needs quarterly and track whether it worked.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:48.225
After that, for reputation, keep bios current, watch for opportunities and escalate

00:17:48.225 --> 00:17:50.450
negative publicity immediately.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:53.925
Finally, don't skip the one that feels least urgent.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:56.188
Think about this one.

00:17:56.188 --> 00:18:00.025
A negative article about the firm appears in a legal trade blog,

00:18:00.025 --> 00:18:02.125
and nobody notices for a week.

00:18:02.125 --> 00:18:05.725
Which procedure should have caught it, and what would it have told you to do?

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:08.525
Quick check.

00:18:08.525 --> 00:18:12.488
Which SOP covers saving certificates in an audit-ready folder?

00:18:12.488 --> 00:18:18.000
Is it A, the Team Upskilling SOP, B, the Attendance Tracking SOP,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:24.550
C, the Event Registration SOP, or D, the Reputation and Recognition SOP?

00:18:25.375 --> 00:18:26.650
Take a second.

00:18:27.425 --> 00:18:30.238
The answer is B, Attendance Tracking.

00:18:30.238 --> 00:18:34.075
That's where completion evidence, like certificates and webinar reports,

00:18:34.075 --> 00:18:35.800
gets filed for audits.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:40.888
Lawyers must complete continuing legal education,

00:18:40.888 --> 00:18:46.325
or CLE, every reporting period, and these deadlines don't get extended.

00:18:46.325 --> 00:18:49.838
A single total for the firm hides who's actually at risk.

00:18:49.838 --> 00:18:55.075
So you track each person: completed hours, remaining hours and their deadline.

00:18:55.875 --> 00:18:59.488
First, flag anyone getting close while there's still time to act.

00:18:59.488 --> 00:19:04.600
Then, check the hours actually count: the right category and the right state.

00:19:06.525 --> 00:19:09.625
After that, give reminders plenty of lead time.

00:19:09.625 --> 00:19:13.450
Finally, check the tracker against the certificates on file.

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:15.462
Picture this.

00:19:15.462 --> 00:19:20.225
Elias has 18 of his 25 hours, still needs 2 ethics hours,

00:19:20.225 --> 00:19:22.650
and his deadline is in seven weeks.

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:26.750
What do you flag today, and what do you check about the hours he already has?

00:19:28.675 --> 00:19:31.475
Good training fits into people's real schedules,

00:19:31.475 --> 00:19:34.738
and it's judged by what people can actually do afterwards,

00:19:34.738 --> 00:19:37.038
not by how many people showed up.

00:19:37.038 --> 00:19:42.025
Attendance tells us the room was full; it doesn't tell us anyone learned anything.

00:19:42.825 --> 00:19:46.525
First, offer online and in-person options where you can.

00:19:46.525 --> 00:19:50.550
Next, decide before the session how you'll judge whether it worked.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:53.825
Then, collect feedback right after the session.

00:19:55.725 --> 00:20:00.100
After that, track results over time, so you can see which formats work.

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:03.625
Finally, use what you learn to improve the next session.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:05.988
Take a second with this one.

00:20:05.988 --> 00:20:09.325
Elias wants to know whether the new billing training worked.

00:20:09.325 --> 00:20:12.313
You have attendance numbers and a feedback survey.

00:20:12.313 --> 00:20:15.075
What's missing, and what can you still measure?

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:21.688
Memberships, clubs and associations all renew at different times of year.

00:20:21.688 --> 00:20:27.163
Relying on the renewal notice, or on memory, is exactly how one quietly lapses,

00:20:27.163 --> 00:20:29.050
usually the one that mattered.

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:35.025
First, build a tracker with the name, expiry date, status and who follows up.

00:20:35.025 --> 00:20:38.463
Next, set reminders well before each expiry.

00:20:38.463 --> 00:20:42.325
Then, automate reminders once there are too many to remember.

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:48.300
After that, check the terms and price haven't changed before renewing.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:52.825
Finally, review the list now and then for memberships nobody uses.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:54.838
Try this one.

00:20:54.838 --> 00:20:59.363
Elias's state bar membership, two practice-section memberships and a country club

00:20:59.363 --> 00:21:04.400
all renew within 60 days, and one card on file expires this month.

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:07.900
Build the tracker rows, and decide what you'd do first.

00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:13.125
Gift rules are stricter than most people's instincts.

00:21:13.125 --> 00:21:16.563
Something that feels like ordinary courtesy with a client,

00:21:16.563 --> 00:21:21.325
vendor or official can still break a rule, or look like an attempt to influence

00:21:21.325 --> 00:21:22.325
someone.

00:21:23.125 --> 00:21:27.675
First, know the firm's gift policy before accepting or offering anything beyond

00:21:27.675 --> 00:21:29.225
small courtesies.

00:21:29.225 --> 00:21:34.200
Next, log any gift above the policy limit, even if it seems fine.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:39.175
Finally, take extra care with government officials and regulated parties.

00:21:39.925 --> 00:21:41.513
Take a second with this one.

00:21:41.513 --> 00:21:45.625
A court-reporting firm offers you two concert tickets 'as thanks for all the

00:21:45.625 --> 00:21:47.800
business.' You'd love to go.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:48.925
What do you do?

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:53.888
You don't need to understand malpractice insurance;

00:21:53.888 --> 00:21:57.163
you need to spot the warning signs and pass them on.

00:21:57.163 --> 00:22:01.713
Catching early signs of a possible claim gives the firm many more options than

00:22:01.713 --> 00:22:03.200
finding out late.

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:08.000
First, know where the insurance documents and renewal dates are kept.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:11.900
Next, if a client's language suggests serious unhappiness,

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:16.200
like mentions of complaints or damages, flag it immediately.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:20.625
Finally, keep renewal and review dates on the compliance calendar.

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:22.938
Here's a situation.

00:22:22.938 --> 00:22:27.925
A client's email says, 'We're considering our options given how this was handled.'

00:22:27.925 --> 00:22:31.600
What's your read, and what do you do beyond replying normally?

00:22:33.525 --> 00:22:36.925
Section 4 of 5: Stress and Wellbeing.

00:22:36.925 --> 00:22:41.000
We'll look at recognizing stress and burnout, techniques that work at a desk,

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.975
and setting boundaries under executive pressure.

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:48.700
A bit of pressure can actually help you focus.

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:53.150
Burnout is different: it's what happens when stress keeps going for weeks with no

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:57.725
relief, until you feel exhausted, detached and less effective.

00:22:57.725 --> 00:23:02.125
This job has its own pressures, like always being on call and carrying other

00:23:02.125 --> 00:23:03.700
people's deadlines.

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:08.050
It builds in stages, from healthy pressure, to strain,

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:12.363
to chronic stress and finally to burnout, and the earlier stages are where it's

00:23:12.363 --> 00:23:14.275
easiest to turn around.

00:23:16.225 --> 00:23:19.625
First, once a week, take two minutes to check in with yourself:

00:23:19.625 --> 00:23:23.300
energy, sleep, mistakes, and anything you keep dreading.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:28.250
Next, name the exact thing causing the stress, like late-night texts or unclear

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:29.488
priorities.

00:23:29.488 --> 00:23:32.275
A specific problem has a specific fix.

00:23:34.225 --> 00:23:38.650
Then, watch the pattern for a couple of weeks before deciding what to change.

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:42.113
Finally, if it lasts for weeks or affects your sleep,

00:23:42.113 --> 00:23:46.250
health or relationships, talk to your manager and consider support,

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:49.700
like an employee assistance program or your doctor.

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:52.063
Take a second with this one.

00:23:52.063 --> 00:23:56.300
You've double-booked Elias twice this week, you're snapping at vendors and you check

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:59.400
email at 11 every night 'just in case'.

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:02.400
What's going on, and what are your first three steps?

00:24:04.325 --> 00:24:07.688
Think about when stress actually hits: mid-afternoon,

00:24:07.688 --> 00:24:10.163
three things due and the phone ringing.

00:24:10.163 --> 00:24:12.813
A weekend away doesn't help you in that moment.

00:24:12.813 --> 00:24:15.825
Something small you can do right where you're sitting does.

00:24:16.625 --> 00:24:20.138
First, before a call you're nervous about, take a few slow breaths:

00:24:20.138 --> 00:24:22.125
count four in and six out.

00:24:22.125 --> 00:24:24.500
When you breathe out more slowly than you breathe in,

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:27.000
your body gets the message to calm down.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:30.100
Next, when your head is spinning, get everything onto paper,

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:32.425
then choose just one next step.

00:24:32.425 --> 00:24:35.225
One step is manageable; twenty isn't.

00:24:37.175 --> 00:24:40.238
Then, build small breaks into the day: stand up,

00:24:40.238 --> 00:24:43.375
stretch or walk for a few minutes every hour or so.

00:24:43.375 --> 00:24:46.688
Finally, end the day on purpose: look at tomorrow,

00:24:46.688 --> 00:24:49.488
write down your top three and close the inbox.

00:24:49.488 --> 00:24:51.650
That's how you actually switch off.

00:24:52.425 --> 00:24:53.838
Think about this one.

00:24:53.838 --> 00:24:59.300
Elias calls in a rush: a court date moved, three meetings have to shift and a family

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:01.200
event now overlaps.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:04.825
What do you do in the first five minutes to keep a clear head before you touch the

00:25:04.825 --> 00:25:05.925
calendar?

00:25:07.825 --> 00:25:12.425
When an executive is stressed, match their problem with calm facts and options,

00:25:12.425 --> 00:25:14.150
not with more stress.

00:25:14.150 --> 00:25:17.325
Boundaries are simply agreements: when you're available,

00:25:17.325 --> 00:25:20.700
how fast you'll reply and what counts as an emergency.

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:23.325
Without them, everything feels urgent.

00:25:24.125 --> 00:25:27.700
First, agree the basics in writing: your working hours,

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:31.625
what's a real after-hours emergency and how to reach you for one.

00:25:31.625 --> 00:25:34.813
Next, when a new request crashes into existing work,

00:25:34.813 --> 00:25:39.700
ask, 'Which of these should move?' instead of silently taking on both.

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:46.475
Finally, under pressure, use a simple pattern: 'I understand this is urgent.

00:25:46.475 --> 00:25:48.400
The filing is due at three.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:51.875
I can move the vendor call, or ask Maria to cover it.'

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:54.313
Take a second with this one.

00:25:54.313 --> 00:25:59.225
Your agreement with Elias says after-hours contact is for court deadlines and family

00:25:59.225 --> 00:26:00.500
emergencies.

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:06.113
At 9:30 p.m. he texts: 'Can you check if the partner lunch is still on Friday?' What

00:26:06.113 --> 00:26:09.100
do you do tonight, and how do you raise it tomorrow?

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:14.150
Raising your workload early, with facts and options,

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:16.475
is professional, not complaining.

00:26:16.475 --> 00:26:20.625
Rest isn't the opposite of good work; it's what makes good work possible.

00:26:20.625 --> 00:26:25.238
And help exists: many employers have confidential support programs,

00:26:25.238 --> 00:26:30.125
and in the US, anyone in crisis can call or text 988.

00:26:30.925 --> 00:26:34.425
First, before the conversation, write down your regular tasks,

00:26:34.425 --> 00:26:39.025
the hours they take, what's slipping and two or three possible fixes.

00:26:39.025 --> 00:26:42.213
Next, when you take time off, hand over properly:

00:26:42.213 --> 00:26:47.125
access for your cover, an out-of-office message and a one-page status note.

00:26:49.075 --> 00:26:52.138
Finally, find out where support is before you need it:

00:26:52.138 --> 00:26:57.025
your employer's program, your manager or HR, and the people you trust.

00:26:57.800 --> 00:26:59.250
Here's one to try.

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:02.300
For two months you've been working 55-hour weeks,

00:27:02.300 --> 00:27:04.313
and you're starting to make mistakes.

00:27:04.313 --> 00:27:08.438
You want to raise it with Elias without sharing personal health details.

00:27:08.438 --> 00:27:13.200
Pause the video and draft your first two sentences, and the options you'd bring.

00:27:15.125 --> 00:27:18.800
Section 5 of 5: Reputation and Brand.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:23.725
We'll cover protecting the brand online, awards and charitable coordination,

00:27:23.725 --> 00:27:26.850
and building an executive's media and speaking kit.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:32.975
When someone posts a bad review, respond calmly and factually,

00:27:32.975 --> 00:27:34.950
and never argue in public.

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:39.763
A negative review, handled well, can actually do more for the firm's reputation than

00:27:39.763 --> 00:27:43.825
ten positive ones, because everyone sees how you respond.

00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:49.275
First, monitor mentions and reviews regularly; don't just wait for trouble.

00:27:49.275 --> 00:27:53.425
Then, apply 'need to know' to anything sensitive that comes up.

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:59.675
After that, draft a considered reply rather than reacting in the moment.

00:27:59.675 --> 00:28:03.350
Finally, escalate anything beyond a routine review,

00:28:03.350 --> 00:28:06.325
like legal threats or coordinated attacks.

00:28:07.075 --> 00:28:08.513
Here's one to try.

00:28:08.513 --> 00:28:13.825
A former client posts a one-star review: 'Thorne & Partners never returned my calls

00:28:13.825 --> 00:28:18.175
and overcharged me.' Pause the video and draft the public reply,

00:28:18.175 --> 00:28:20.975
then decide what you'd check before posting it.

00:28:22.875 --> 00:28:23.863
Quick check.

00:28:23.863 --> 00:28:26.888
A client posts an inaccurate negative review.

00:28:26.888 --> 00:28:28.550
What's the best response?

00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:31.538
Is it A, argue publicly to prove the client wrong,

00:28:31.538 --> 00:28:34.775
B, delete or hide the review without responding,

00:28:34.775 --> 00:28:39.050
C, reply professionally and factually, without escalating the conflict,

00:28:39.050 --> 00:28:42.100
or D, ignore it and hope it disappears?

00:28:42.925 --> 00:28:44.200
Take a second.

00:28:44.975 --> 00:28:45.475
It's C.

00:28:45.413 --> 00:28:50.250
A calm, factual public response protects the brand better than silence or

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:51.650
confrontation.

00:28:53.575 --> 00:28:58.113
Great nominations get missed simply because nobody tracked the deadline.

00:28:58.113 --> 00:29:04.150
Award applications have exact eligibility, exact materials and a firm deadline.

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:08.375
Charitable commitments need the same care, because a promise isn't the same as a

00:29:08.375 --> 00:29:10.925
donation made and documented.

00:29:11.725 --> 00:29:15.125
First, keep a simple tracker of awards worth pursuing:

00:29:15.125 --> 00:29:19.175
name, deadline, eligibility, materials and status.

00:29:19.175 --> 00:29:23.513
Next, confirm the criteria and materials well before the deadline.

00:29:23.513 --> 00:29:27.625
Then, for charitable pledges, track both the commitment and whether it was

00:29:27.625 --> 00:29:28.775
fulfilled.

00:29:30.725 --> 00:29:36.100
After that, keep records of donations, because they often matter for tax purposes.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:40.625
Finally, treat awards and charity as proactive reputation-building.

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:42.613
Picture this.

00:29:42.613 --> 00:29:48.750
Elias pledged $5,000 to a legal aid gala and was nominated for a regional bar award

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:50.313
due in three weeks.

00:29:50.313 --> 00:29:54.100
What goes in the tracker for each, and what documents do you keep?

00:29:56.075 --> 00:30:01.200
A ready media kit turns a last-minute press request into a same-day reply.

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:03.963
It's a folder with the executive's current bio,

00:30:03.963 --> 00:30:07.650
a good headshot, key talking points and past coverage,

00:30:07.650 --> 00:30:09.975
and it's only useful if it's current.

00:30:10.775 --> 00:30:14.313
First, keep a standing folder with a short and a long bio,

00:30:14.313 --> 00:30:17.350
a recent headshot and a one-page summary.

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:21.600
Next, keep a running list of past media and speaking engagements.

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:25.250
Finally, review and refresh the kit every quarter.

00:30:26.025 --> 00:30:27.338
Think about this one.

00:30:27.338 --> 00:30:31.225
A journalist needs Elias's bio and headshot in two hours,

00:30:31.225 --> 00:30:34.713
and the bio on file lists a role he no longer holds.

00:30:34.713 --> 00:30:36.950
What do you do, given the deadline?

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:40.288
Now it's your turn.

00:30:40.288 --> 00:30:45.100
In today's Practice Lab, the Events, CLE and Reputation Tracker,

00:30:45.100 --> 00:30:49.863
you'll write the invitation and registration form for a firm CLE seminar,

00:30:49.863 --> 00:30:54.750
catch unconfirmed registrants, turn a strategy call into clean meeting notes,

00:30:54.750 --> 00:31:00.200
spot who's behind on CLE hours, answer a negative review and track which awards

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:02.100
deadlines are still open.

